---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import MobileCta from "../components/MobileCta.astro";
import EarMark from "../components/EarMark.astro";
import { SITE, LINKEDIN, APP_URL, profilePage, breadcrumbList } from "../lib/schema";

const title = "About the author: Mahipal, developer of Clarive";
const description =
  "Mahipal is an iOS product engineer and the developer of Clarive, a listening device app for iPhone. Who writes these articles, and what they're based on.";

const bio =
  "iOS product engineer and the developer of Clarive, a listening device app for iPhone that combines real-time audio amplification with live captions.";

const jsonLd = [
  profilePage("Mahipal", bio),
  breadcrumbList([
    { name: "Home", url: `${SITE}/` },
    { name: "About", url: `${SITE}/about/` },
  ]),
];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/about" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Nav />

  <main class="legal px-safe">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
      <span
        class="grid h-14 w-14 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-[1rem] bg-gradient-crimson text-white glow-crimson"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
        <EarMark size={30} />
      </span>
      <div>
        <h1 class="!text-[2rem]">Mahipal</h1>
        <p class="updated !mt-1">iOS Product Engineer · Developer of Clarive</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      I build <a href="/">Clarive</a>, an iOS app that turns an iPhone and any headphones into
      a real-time listening device: audio amplification plus live captions, processed entirely on
      the device. I also write the articles on this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Why there's a name on these articles</h2>
    <p>
      Hearing is close enough to health that it matters who's telling you things. So: I'm a
      software engineer, not a clinician. What I can speak to with any authority is how iPhone
      audio actually behaves: how the microphone, headphone routing, and Apple's on-device speech
      recognition perform in real rooms, because I've spent a lot of time shipping against them and
      watching them fail in specific ways.
    </p>
    <p>
      What I can't speak to is anything clinical. I can't tell you what your symptoms mean, whether
      you need a hearing aid, or how to interpret a hearing test. When an article gets near that
      line, it says so and points you to someone qualified. Anything about how hearing works cites
      a real source, usually the <a href="https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIDCD</a>
      or the <a href="https://www.who.int/health-topics/hearing-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO</a>,
      rather than my own say-so.
    </p>

    <h2>How I write these</h2>
    <p>
      Every article starts from a question people actually search for, and answers it in the first
      few lines instead of making you scroll. Anything I say about Clarive is checked against what
      the app really does. Where I compare it to other apps, I say up front that I built one of
      them, and I point out where the others are better.
    </p>

    <h2>One thing to be clear about</h2>
    <p>
      Clarive is a sound amplification app, not a hearing aid or medical device. It doesn't
      diagnose or treat hearing loss. If you're having trouble hearing, see an audiologist or your
      doctor.
    </p>

    <div class="contact-card">
      <h2 class="!mt-0">Elsewhere</h2>
      <p class="contact-email">
        <a href={LINKEDIN} target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a> ·
        <a href={APP_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clarive on the App Store</a> ·
        <a href="/contact-us/">Contact</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
  <MobileCta />
</Base>
